window.docContents[55].resolve({"/Build-Tools-and-Distribution/Lake/#lake-cache-services":{"contents":"A cache service is a named remote server that supplies both build artifacts and mappings.\nA service named \"reservoir\" that provides cached data from Reservoir is always available.\nAdditional cache services can be defined in the system's Lake configuration file and in packages.\nThe --service option selects a named cache service when using one of Lake's cache commands.\n\nDefining a cache service in a package enables package authors to provide pre-built artifacts to users.\nServices defined in the system's configuration file can support both reading with cache get and writing with cache put, while services defined in packages are read-only.\n\nThe available cache services can be queried using cache services.\nIf there are duplicate names, cache service names passed to --service are resolved by consulting the following sources in order:0. the name \"reservoir\" always points to Reservoir1. the system Lake configuration file2. the root package's Lake configuration file3. direct dependencies, with later require statements taking precedence4. transitive dependencies, in increasing depth order, with later dependencies taking precedence over earlier dependencies\n\nRunning cache get with the --service option causes Lake to fetch both the mappings file and the build artifacts that the service provides.\nThe --mappings-only flag causes it to fetch only the mappings file and record that the artifacts it describes are available from the service.\nIf build discovers that a desired artifact is available on a service but not present, it downloads it when it is needed.\nTo use an S3-based service instead of Reservoir, a cache get command should specify the following options:* --service to specify the service name (optional if cache.defaultService is set or if --package is provided for a package that configures a download cache service)* --scope for the cache's scope, which the service provider should advertise to users when the cache service uses scopes* optionally --mappings-only, which delays the download of artifacts until they are required for a build* optionally --package, to fetch mappings and artifacts for a package other than the root package.\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Build Tools\u0009Lake\u0009Caching Builds\u0009Remote Artifact Caches","header":"25.1.3.2.2. Cache Services","id":"/Build-Tools-and-Distribution/Lake/#lake-cache-services"},"/IO/Tasks-and-Threads/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--IO--Tasks-and-Threads--Task-Results":{"contents":"Blocks the current thread until the given task has finished execution, and then returns the result\nof the task. If the current thread is itself executing a (non-dedicated) task, the maximum\nthreadpool size is temporarily increased by one while waiting so as to ensure the process cannot\nbe deadlocked by threadpool starvation. Note that when the current thread is unblocked, more tasks\nthan the configured threadpool size may temporarily be running at the same time until sufficiently\nmany tasks have finished.Task.map and Task.bind should be preferred over Task.get for setting up task dependencies\nwhere possible as they do not require temporarily growing the threadpool in this way. In\nparticular, calling Task.get in a task continuation with (sync := true) will panic as the\ncontinuation is decidedly not \"cheap\" in this case and deadlocks may otherwise occur. The\nwaited-upon task should instead be returned and unwrapped using Task.bind/IO.bindTask.\n\nWaits for the task to finish, then returns its result.\n\nWaits until any of the tasks in the list has finished, then returns its result.\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009IO\u0009Tasks and Threads","header":"22.11.2. Task Results","id":"/IO/Tasks-and-Threads/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--IO--Tasks-and-Threads--Task-Results"},"/Run-Time-Code/Boxing/#boxing":{"contents":"Lean values may be represented at runtime in two ways:* Boxed values may be pointers to heap values or require shifting and masking.* Unboxed values are immediately available.\n\nBoxed values are either a pointer to an object, in which case the lowest-order bit is 0, or an immediate value, in which case the lowest-order bit is 1 and the value is found by shifting the representation to the right by one bit.\n\nTypes with an unboxed representation, such as UInt8 and enum inductive types, are represented as the corresponding C types in contexts where the compiler can be sure that the value has said type.\nIn some contexts, such as generic container types like Array, otherwise-unboxed values must be boxed prior to storage.\nIn other words, Bool.not is called with and returns unboxed uint8_t values because the enum inductive type Bool has an unboxed representation, but the individual Bool values in an Array Bool are boxed.\nA field of type Bool in an inductive type's constructor is represented unboxed, while Bools stored in polymorphic fields that are instantiated as Bool are boxed.\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Run-Time Code","header":"12.1. Boxing","id":"/Run-Time-Code/Boxing/#boxing"},"/releases/v4.26.0/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Release-Notes--Lean-4___26___0-_LPAR_2025-12-13_RPAR_--Highlights":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Release Notes\u0009Lean 4.26.0 (2025-12-13)","header":"Highlights","id":"/releases/v4.26.0/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Release-Notes--Lean-4___26___0-_LPAR_2025-12-13_RPAR_--Highlights"},"/releases/v4.30.0/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Release-Notes--Lean-4___30___0-_LPAR_2026-05-26_RPAR_--Highlights--Library-Highlights--HTTP-Library":{"contents":"#12126, #12127, #12128, and #12144 introduce core HTTP data types: Request, Response, Status, Version, Method, Headers, URI, and streaming Body. This is the foundation of a standard HTTP library in Lean.\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Release Notes\u0009Lean 4.30.0 (2026-05-26)\u0009Highlights\u0009Library Highlights","header":"HTTP Library","id":"/releases/v4.30.0/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Release-Notes--Lean-4___30___0-_LPAR_2026-05-26_RPAR_--Highlights--Library-Highlights--HTTP-Library"}});